[
 {
  "title": "Why is my PyTorch training so slow on the GPU?",
  "create_time": 1762197120.0,
  "update_time": 1762198020.0,
  "mapping": {
   "8c815fd7-2382-220f-c4bc-a894a3014467": {
    "id": "8c815fd7-2382-220f-c4bc-a894a3014467",
    "message": null,
    "parent": null,
    "children": [
     "aaaf2ac2-2f71-849e-4dda-60ff14337606"
    ]
   },
   "aaaf2ac2-2f71-849e-4dda-60ff14337606": {
    "id": "aaaf2ac2-2f71-849e-4dda-60ff14337606",
    "message": {
     "id": "aaaf2ac2-2f71-849e-4dda-60ff14337606",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1762197120.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "My PyTorch training loop runs at like 40% GPU utilization on an RTX 4070. The model is a small transformer, batch size 32. What should I check first?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "8c815fd7-2382-220f-c4bc-a894a3014467",
    "children": [
     "d0b9edf3-7508-1406-3887-bee10d8b2afb"
    ]
   },
   "d0b9edf3-7508-1406-3887-bee10d8b2afb": {
    "id": "d0b9edf3-7508-1406-3887-bee10d8b2afb",
    "message": {
     "id": "d0b9edf3-7508-1406-3887-bee10d8b2afb",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1762197300.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Low GPU utilization in PyTorch usually means the GPU is waiting for data. Check the DataLoader first: set num_workers to 4-8 and pin_memory=True so batches are copied to CUDA memory asynchronously. Second, make sure you are not calling .item() or printing the loss every step, because that forces a CUDA synchronization. Third, use torch.profiler to see whether time goes to the data pipeline or to CUDA kernels. If kernels dominate, enable mixed precision with torch.autocast and bfloat16; it uses the Tensor Cores and roughly doubles throughput on Ada GPUs."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "aaaf2ac2-2f71-849e-4dda-60ff14337606",
    "children": [
     "3d2dfa46-ac6b-4168-1785-9b4fcf8208ac"
    ]
   },
   "3d2dfa46-ac6b-4168-1785-9b4fcf8208ac": {
    "id": "3d2dfa46-ac6b-4168-1785-9b4fcf8208ac",
    "message": {
     "id": "3d2dfa46-ac6b-4168-1785-9b4fcf8208ac",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1762197480.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Profiler says a lot of time is in tiny CUDA kernels, like layer norm and GELU launching separately."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "d0b9edf3-7508-1406-3887-bee10d8b2afb",
    "children": [
     "8a78dc48-3b4d-9c9f-cafe-1f9c096ff769"
    ]
   },
   "8a78dc48-3b4d-9c9f-cafe-1f9c096ff769": {
    "id": "8a78dc48-3b4d-9c9f-cafe-1f9c096ff769",
    "message": {
     "id": "8a78dc48-3b4d-9c9f-cafe-1f9c096ff769",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1762197660.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "That is kernel launch overhead. torch.compile fuses those elementwise ops into larger kernels generated by Triton, so one fused kernel replaces five small ones. Try model = torch.compile(model) with mode='max-autotune'. Kernel fusion is the main reason torch.compile accelerates small transformers. If you still see gaps between kernels, CUDA Graphs (mode='reduce-overhead') remove the launch overhead entirely by replaying a captured graph."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "3d2dfa46-ac6b-4168-1785-9b4fcf8208ac",
    "children": [
     "87dfc919-5bc5-0fb7-af2c-85f72aa2525f"
    ]
   },
   "87dfc919-5bc5-0fb7-af2c-85f72aa2525f": {
    "id": "87dfc919-5bc5-0fb7-af2c-85f72aa2525f",
    "message": {
     "id": "87dfc919-5bc5-0fb7-af2c-85f72aa2525f",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1762197840.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "torch.compile took me from 40% to 85% utilization. Nice. Is Triton something I should learn directly?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "8a78dc48-3b4d-9c9f-cafe-1f9c096ff769",
    "children": [
     "7bca81a9-87f3-1ce4-6870-b0809026cb93"
    ]
   },
   "7bca81a9-87f3-1ce4-6870-b0809026cb93": {
    "id": "7bca81a9-87f3-1ce4-6870-b0809026cb93",
    "message": {
     "id": "7bca81a9-87f3-1ce4-6870-b0809026cb93",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1762198020.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Worth it if you want custom fused kernels. Triton is a Python DSL that compiles to GPU code; you write block-level programs and Triton handles memory coalescing and shared memory. A good first project is a fused softmax or a fused layer norm. FlashAttention-style attention kernels are also commonly written in Triton today."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "87dfc919-5bc5-0fb7-af2c-85f72aa2525f",
    "children": []
   }
  },
  "current_node": "7bca81a9-87f3-1ce4-6870-b0809026cb93",
  "conversation_id": "5dc15af4-477e-7831-7158-8c6b2157d4c8"
 },
 {
  "title": "Quantization basics: int8 vs 4-bit for LLM inference",
  "create_time": 1764357120.0,
  "update_time": 1764358020.0,
  "mapping": {
   "f89a72b7-20ce-a6e0-6ab3-667b9febe404": {
    "id": "f89a72b7-20ce-a6e0-6ab3-667b9febe404",
    "message": null,
    "parent": null,
    "children": [
     "90ebbb51-9837-3e6d-be0a-cabcbd229c08"
    ]
   },
   "90ebbb51-9837-3e6d-be0a-cabcbd229c08": {
    "id": "90ebbb51-9837-3e6d-be0a-cabcbd229c08",
    "message": {
     "id": "90ebbb51-9837-3e6d-be0a-cabcbd229c08",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1764357120.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Explain quantization for LLMs. When do I use int8 and when 4-bit like GPTQ or AWQ?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "f89a72b7-20ce-a6e0-6ab3-667b9febe404",
    "children": [
     "0345ae66-b89b-d618-5c88-f9d8986e46ae"
    ]
   },
   "0345ae66-b89b-d618-5c88-f9d8986e46ae": {
    "id": "0345ae66-b89b-d618-5c88-f9d8986e46ae",
    "message": {
     "id": "0345ae66-b89b-d618-5c88-f9d8986e46ae",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1764357300.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Quantization stores weights with fewer bits to save memory and bandwidth. int8 weight quantization (LLM.int8, or SmoothQuant for weights and activations) is nearly lossless and halves memory versus fp16. 4-bit methods compress 4x: GPTQ quantizes layer by layer using second-order information, while AWQ protects the salient weight channels identified from activation statistics. For a 7B model on a 12 GB GPU, 4-bit AWQ or GPTQ is the practical choice. Quantization mostly accelerates decoding because token generation is memory-bandwidth bound."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "90ebbb51-9837-3e6d-be0a-cabcbd229c08",
    "children": [
     "993be7ad-3850-ee5b-4580-e03ee175a8ed"
    ]
   },
   "993be7ad-3850-ee5b-4580-e03ee175a8ed": {
    "id": "993be7ad-3850-ee5b-4580-e03ee175a8ed",
    "message": {
     "id": "993be7ad-3850-ee5b-4580-e03ee175a8ed",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1764357480.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Does quantization hurt quality a lot?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "0345ae66-b89b-d618-5c88-f9d8986e46ae",
    "children": [
     "926abadf-c7a4-69b1-e10a-16ba9b925a3a"
    ]
   },
   "926abadf-c7a4-69b1-e10a-16ba9b925a3a": {
    "id": "926abadf-c7a4-69b1-e10a-16ba9b925a3a",
    "message": {
     "id": "926abadf-c7a4-69b1-e10a-16ba9b925a3a",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1764357660.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "At 8 bits, barely. At 4 bits you typically lose a little perplexity; AWQ and GPTQ with group size 128 keep it small. Below 4 bits quality drops quickly unless you use quantization-aware training. Always evaluate on your own task, not only on perplexity."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "993be7ad-3850-ee5b-4580-e03ee175a8ed",
    "children": [
     "31430a63-f5dd-e344-9de7-80fd9d3c5833"
    ]
   },
   "31430a63-f5dd-e344-9de7-80fd9d3c5833": {
    "id": "31430a63-f5dd-e344-9de7-80fd9d3c5833",
    "message": {
     "id": "31430a63-f5dd-e344-9de7-80fd9d3c5833",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1764357840.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Which inference server supports AWQ?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "926abadf-c7a4-69b1-e10a-16ba9b925a3a",
    "children": [
     "df7b263b-446c-ff50-0850-2382db5c2404"
    ]
   },
   "df7b263b-446c-ff50-0850-2382db5c2404": {
    "id": "df7b263b-446c-ff50-0850-2382db5c2404",
    "message": {
     "id": "df7b263b-446c-ff50-0850-2382db5c2404",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1764358020.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "vLLM supports AWQ and GPTQ checkpoints directly, and so do llama.cpp (via GGUF quant formats) and TensorRT-LLM. vLLM is the easiest if you want an OpenAI-compatible API server on a GPU."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "31430a63-f5dd-e344-9de7-80fd9d3c5833",
    "children": []
   }
  },
  "current_node": "df7b263b-446c-ff50-0850-2382db5c2404",
  "conversation_id": "8c55370e-23c4-21d9-220b-07aefa7ae967"
 },
 {
  "title": "Choosing an embedding model for RAG",
  "create_time": 1767985920.0,
  "update_time": 1767986820.0,
  "mapping": {
   "39cbae26-1004-da4b-8f4e-d36e5b16a446": {
    "id": "39cbae26-1004-da4b-8f4e-d36e5b16a446",
    "message": null,
    "parent": null,
    "children": [
     "0dd2d285-4304-43a1-7ee4-f47e44b38933"
    ]
   },
   "0dd2d285-4304-43a1-7ee4-f47e44b38933": {
    "id": "0dd2d285-4304-43a1-7ee4-f47e44b38933",
    "message": {
     "id": "0dd2d285-4304-43a1-7ee4-f47e44b38933",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1767985920.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "I'm building retrieval-augmented generation over my notes. Which embedding model should I use?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "39cbae26-1004-da4b-8f4e-d36e5b16a446",
    "children": [
     "623cdcf5-e5c7-0953-6ef6-1debd3e38fca"
    ]
   },
   "623cdcf5-e5c7-0953-6ef6-1debd3e38fca": {
    "id": "623cdcf5-e5c7-0953-6ef6-1debd3e38fca",
    "message": {
     "id": "623cdcf5-e5c7-0953-6ef6-1debd3e38fca",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1767986100.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "For English notes on a laptop, BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5 is a strong default: 384 dimensions, fast on CPU, good MTEB retrieval scores. bge-base or e5-base are better if you have a GPU. Use sentence-transformers to compute embeddings, normalize them, and use cosine similarity. For bge, prefix queries with the instruction 'Represent this sentence for searching relevant passages:'."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "0dd2d285-4304-43a1-7ee4-f47e44b38933",
    "children": [
     "c0f47f6f-5dba-c108-ef5f-5731d29ae3b0"
    ]
   },
   "c0f47f6f-5dba-c108-ef5f-5731d29ae3b0": {
    "id": "c0f47f6f-5dba-c108-ef5f-5731d29ae3b0",
    "message": {
     "id": "c0f47f6f-5dba-c108-ef5f-5731d29ae3b0",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1767986280.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "How should I chunk the notes?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "623cdcf5-e5c7-0953-6ef6-1debd3e38fca",
    "children": [
     "c135f324-a5bc-b72b-c312-0ffec24d72ce"
    ]
   },
   "c135f324-a5bc-b72b-c312-0ffec24d72ce": {
    "id": "c135f324-a5bc-b72b-c312-0ffec24d72ce",
    "message": {
     "id": "c135f324-a5bc-b72b-c312-0ffec24d72ce",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1767986460.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Chunk on structure first (headings, paragraphs) and aim for 200-500 tokens per chunk with a little overlap. For chat transcripts, keep a question together with its answer: the user's wording matches future queries and the answer carries the details. Store metadata like date and source with each chunk; it helps filtering and citations."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "c0f47f6f-5dba-c108-ef5f-5731d29ae3b0",
    "children": [
     "3308ae1a-edcb-9e56-d112-a008a166e8c8"
    ]
   },
   "3308ae1a-edcb-9e56-d112-a008a166e8c8": {
    "id": "3308ae1a-edcb-9e56-d112-a008a166e8c8",
    "message": {
     "id": "3308ae1a-edcb-9e56-d112-a008a166e8c8",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1767986640.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "And where do I store the vectors?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "c135f324-a5bc-b72b-c312-0ffec24d72ce",
    "children": [
     "18204df8-9fa0-9a3b-233c-78facd177ee4"
    ]
   },
   "18204df8-9fa0-9a3b-233c-78facd177ee4": {
    "id": "18204df8-9fa0-9a3b-233c-78facd177ee4",
    "message": {
     "id": "18204df8-9fa0-9a3b-233c-78facd177ee4",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1767986820.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "For under a million chunks, FAISS with a flat inner-product index is exact and fast enough. Beyond that, use an HNSW or IVF index. Chroma or Qdrant are alternatives if you want a server with metadata filtering, but FAISS is the simplest for a local-first tool."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "3308ae1a-edcb-9e56-d112-a008a166e8c8",
    "children": []
   }
  },
  "current_node": "18204df8-9fa0-9a3b-233c-78facd177ee4",
  "conversation_id": "56dccb73-f397-9a35-aaeb-745fb6db22e8"
 },
 {
  "title": "BM25 vs embeddings: is hybrid search worth it?",
  "create_time": 1770059520.0,
  "update_time": 1770060420.0,
  "mapping": {
   "e99ae5b7-3be8-b0a4-e78b-e9c9f260a042": {
    "id": "e99ae5b7-3be8-b0a4-e78b-e9c9f260a042",
    "message": null,
    "parent": null,
    "children": [
     "6f6ac603-d701-8a1e-43a4-646d6228ca68"
    ]
   },
   "6f6ac603-d701-8a1e-43a4-646d6228ca68": {
    "id": "6f6ac603-d701-8a1e-43a4-646d6228ca68",
    "message": {
     "id": "6f6ac603-d701-8a1e-43a4-646d6228ca68",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1770059520.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Do I really need BM25 if I have good embeddings?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "e99ae5b7-3be8-b0a4-e78b-e9c9f260a042",
    "children": [
     "b68d175a-cb18-a062-38c6-134bc335f61c"
    ]
   },
   "b68d175a-cb18-a062-38c6-134bc335f61c": {
    "id": "b68d175a-cb18-a062-38c6-134bc335f61c",
    "message": {
     "id": "b68d175a-cb18-a062-38c6-134bc335f61c",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1770059700.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "For personal knowledge bases, yes. Queries often contain exact names: library names, error messages, repo names. Embeddings blur those; BM25 matches them exactly. Hybrid search runs both and fuses the rankings, typically with reciprocal rank fusion (RRF), which needs no score calibration. On most benchmarks hybrid beats either alone."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "6f6ac603-d701-8a1e-43a4-646d6228ca68",
    "children": [
     "997a0116-9184-1857-e9d5-c3e34ec767bf"
    ]
   },
   "997a0116-9184-1857-e9d5-c3e34ec767bf": {
    "id": "997a0116-9184-1857-e9d5-c3e34ec767bf",
    "message": {
     "id": "997a0116-9184-1857-e9d5-c3e34ec767bf",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1770059880.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "How do I evaluate whether my retrieval is good?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "b68d175a-cb18-a062-38c6-134bc335f61c",
    "children": [
     "c2d9b1b1-87be-3ecd-2bad-4ea789d4da04"
    ]
   },
   "c2d9b1b1-87be-3ecd-2bad-4ea789d4da04": {
    "id": "c2d9b1b1-87be-3ecd-2bad-4ea789d4da04",
    "message": {
     "id": "c2d9b1b1-87be-3ecd-2bad-4ea789d4da04",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1770060060.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Build a small benchmark: 30-50 real questions, each labeled with the documents that answer them. Measure Recall@K (did the right document appear in the top K), MRR (how high it ranked) and nDCG. Run ablations: BM25 only, dense only, hybrid. Write the questions from memory, the way you would actually search, otherwise you test with wording copied from the documents and the numbers look too good."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "997a0116-9184-1857-e9d5-c3e34ec767bf",
    "children": [
     "ef988657-854e-b41a-ccaf-59da7a2d1f25"
    ]
   },
   "ef988657-854e-b41a-ccaf-59da7a2d1f25": {
    "id": "ef988657-854e-b41a-ccaf-59da7a2d1f25",
    "message": {
     "id": "ef988657-854e-b41a-ccaf-59da7a2d1f25",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1770060240.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "What about a cross-encoder reranker?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "c2d9b1b1-87be-3ecd-2bad-4ea789d4da04",
    "children": [
     "6a4f2731-54cd-8860-9383-51ac8cc50d0a"
    ]
   },
   "6a4f2731-54cd-8860-9383-51ac8cc50d0a": {
    "id": "6a4f2731-54cd-8860-9383-51ac8cc50d0a",
    "message": {
     "id": "6a4f2731-54cd-8860-9383-51ac8cc50d0a",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1770060420.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "A cross-encoder like bge-reranker-base rescores the top 50 candidates jointly with the query; it usually gives a solid boost to precision at the top. It costs latency, so apply it only to the shortlist."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "ef988657-854e-b41a-ccaf-59da7a2d1f25",
    "children": []
   }
  },
  "current_node": "6a4f2731-54cd-8860-9383-51ac8cc50d0a",
  "conversation_id": "480c6145-3d66-50b9-12bc-37b28615670d"
 },
 {
  "title": "Obsidian as a knowledge base for LLM chat exports",
  "create_time": 1772737920.0,
  "update_time": 1772738460.0,
  "mapping": {
   "30382d9b-c5e5-4294-9780-090d94a37efa": {
    "id": "30382d9b-c5e5-4294-9780-090d94a37efa",
    "message": null,
    "parent": null,
    "children": [
     "5ca5f039-63fa-8059-40a2-f75e9abb9a13"
    ]
   },
   "5ca5f039-63fa-8059-40a2-f75e9abb9a13": {
    "id": "5ca5f039-63fa-8059-40a2-f75e9abb9a13",
    "message": {
     "id": "5ca5f039-63fa-8059-40a2-f75e9abb9a13",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1772737920.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "I want to turn my ChatGPT and Claude exports into an Obsidian vault with backlinks. Good idea?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "30382d9b-c5e5-4294-9780-090d94a37efa",
    "children": [
     "e11e2b52-24fe-2643-40d1-f5dd03ae41b1"
    ]
   },
   "e11e2b52-24fe-2643-40d1-f5dd03ae41b1": {
    "id": "e11e2b52-24fe-2643-40d1-f5dd03ae41b1",
    "message": {
     "id": "e11e2b52-24fe-2643-40d1-f5dd03ae41b1",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1772738100.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Good idea. Obsidian is local-first markdown with backlinks and a graph view, so the vault is both a human interface and a machine-readable store. Convert each conversation to a markdown note with YAML frontmatter (platform, date, title), extract entities and topics, and insert wiki links like [[faiss|FAISS]]. Entity notes then collect backlinks automatically."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "5ca5f039-63fa-8059-40a2-f75e9abb9a13",
    "children": [
     "82f50d6d-32de-bc43-3a85-88a644211ecd"
    ]
   },
   "82f50d6d-32de-bc43-3a85-88a644211ecd": {
    "id": "82f50d6d-32de-bc43-3a85-88a644211ecd",
    "message": {
     "id": "82f50d6d-32de-bc43-3a85-88a644211ecd",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1772738280.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "The Obsidian graph view becomes a hairball with thousands of nodes."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "e11e2b52-24fe-2643-40d1-f5dd03ae41b1",
    "children": [
     "1572970d-e949-9a56-b164-e385e28d10f7"
    ]
   },
   "1572970d-e949-9a56-b164-e385e28d10f7": {
    "id": "1572970d-e949-9a56-b164-e385e28d10f7",
    "message": {
     "id": "1572970d-e949-9a56-b164-e385e28d10f7",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1772738460.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "That is the classic problem. Filter the graph to important nodes, color by folder, and use local graph views. For a custom explorer, show only the top nodes by PageRank first and load neighborhoods on demand when a node is clicked, instead of rendering everything at once."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "82f50d6d-32de-bc43-3a85-88a644211ecd",
    "children": []
   }
  },
  "current_node": "1572970d-e949-9a56-b164-e385e28d10f7",
  "conversation_id": "df2e29b8-77f3-11c1-4de2-b6553b175514"
 },
 {
  "title": "ESP32 e-paper weather station: partial refresh ghosting",
  "create_time": 1763665920.0,
  "update_time": 1763666820.0,
  "mapping": {
   "c15844a1-5482-5046-4737-4f19709f9856": {
    "id": "c15844a1-5482-5046-4737-4f19709f9856",
    "message": null,
    "parent": null,
    "children": [
     "a342bf52-4eb6-fa92-a0e2-f8b19c543266"
    ]
   },
   "a342bf52-4eb6-fa92-a0e2-f8b19c543266": {
    "id": "a342bf52-4eb6-fa92-a0e2-f8b19c543266",
    "message": {
     "id": "a342bf52-4eb6-fa92-a0e2-f8b19c543266",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1763665920.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "I'm building a weather station with an ESP32 and a 1.54 inch e-paper display using GxEPD2. Partial refresh leaves ghosting. How do I fix it?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "c15844a1-5482-5046-4737-4f19709f9856",
    "children": [
     "bdd7e67a-7b86-a718-8db6-afd771cec7cd"
    ]
   },
   "bdd7e67a-7b86-a718-8db6-afd771cec7cd": {
    "id": "bdd7e67a-7b86-a718-8db6-afd771cec7cd",
    "message": {
     "id": "bdd7e67a-7b86-a718-8db6-afd771cec7cd",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1763666100.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Ghosting is expected with repeated partial updates on e-paper. Do a full refresh every N partial updates (for example every 10), and call display.setPartialWindow only for the region that changes. With GxEPD2, use display.firstPage()/nextPage() for paged drawing, and make sure you call display.hibernate() after drawing so the panel is not left powered. Ghosting also gets worse in cold temperatures."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "a342bf52-4eb6-fa92-a0e2-f8b19c543266",
    "children": [
     "d1ec9f51-347e-3b2b-7e49-96cd8c43ca65"
    ]
   },
   "d1ec9f51-347e-3b2b-7e49-96cd8c43ca65": {
    "id": "d1ec9f51-347e-3b2b-7e49-96cd8c43ca65",
    "message": {
     "id": "d1ec9f51-347e-3b2b-7e49-96cd8c43ca65",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1763666280.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "The ESP32 drains my battery in two days."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "bdd7e67a-7b86-a718-8db6-afd771cec7cd",
    "children": [
     "8157299e-54d7-fa98-1be1-28853ef99eff"
    ]
   },
   "8157299e-54d7-fa98-1be1-28853ef99eff": {
    "id": "8157299e-54d7-fa98-1be1-28853ef99eff",
    "message": {
     "id": "8157299e-54d7-fa98-1be1-28853ef99eff",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1763666460.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Use deep sleep between updates: esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup for the refresh interval, and keep Wi-Fi off except when fetching the weather. A typical cycle wakes, connects, fetches JSON over HTTPS, draws a partial update, hibernates the display and goes back to deep sleep. That brings average current into the tens of microamps and battery life into months."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "d1ec9f51-347e-3b2b-7e49-96cd8c43ca65",
    "children": [
     "f6c4c2d6-50da-c0a5-cc44-9f9ac8019e0c"
    ]
   },
   "f6c4c2d6-50da-c0a5-cc44-9f9ac8019e0c": {
    "id": "f6c4c2d6-50da-c0a5-cc44-9f9ac8019e0c",
    "message": {
     "id": "f6c4c2d6-50da-c0a5-cc44-9f9ac8019e0c",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1763666640.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "How do I fetch the weather data?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "8157299e-54d7-fa98-1be1-28853ef99eff",
    "children": [
     "ffa9ca49-a8a5-d410-dfbd-6dd6b9ae94d4"
    ]
   },
   "ffa9ca49-a8a5-d410-dfbd-6dd6b9ae94d4": {
    "id": "ffa9ca49-a8a5-d410-dfbd-6dd6b9ae94d4",
    "message": {
     "id": "ffa9ca49-a8a5-d410-dfbd-6dd6b9ae94d4",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1763666820.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Call a weather API with HTTPClient and parse it with ArduinoJson. Or have your own small backend aggregate the data and return a compact JSON tailored to the display, which keeps the firmware simple and the payload small."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "f6c4c2d6-50da-c0a5-cc44-9f9ac8019e0c",
    "children": []
   }
  },
  "current_node": "ffa9ca49-a8a5-d410-dfbd-6dd6b9ae94d4",
  "conversation_id": "60277f05-f2f8-cc97-f6a3-b126da5cdccb"
 },
 {
  "title": "TinyML: running a quantized model on the ESP32",
  "create_time": 1769368320.0,
  "update_time": 1769368860.0,
  "mapping": {
   "66d8cde3-0738-f088-47fa-b840518bb168": {
    "id": "66d8cde3-0738-f088-47fa-b840518bb168",
    "message": null,
    "parent": null,
    "children": [
     "1a26e245-8f9f-fdf7-b8af-fa7e45c8c32e"
    ]
   },
   "1a26e245-8f9f-fdf7-b8af-fa7e45c8c32e": {
    "id": "1a26e245-8f9f-fdf7-b8af-fa7e45c8c32e",
    "message": {
     "id": "1a26e245-8f9f-fdf7-b8af-fa7e45c8c32e",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1769368320.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Can I run a small neural network on an ESP32-S3 for keyword spotting?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "66d8cde3-0738-f088-47fa-b840518bb168",
    "children": [
     "14c78a12-7272-9891-50a2-d5e78245b46d"
    ]
   },
   "14c78a12-7272-9891-50a2-d5e78245b46d": {
    "id": "14c78a12-7272-9891-50a2-d5e78245b46d",
    "message": {
     "id": "14c78a12-7272-9891-50a2-d5e78245b46d",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1769368500.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Yes. Train a small CNN on MFCC features, then convert it with TensorFlow Lite and apply full int8 quantization; quantization is what makes it fit in the S3's memory and run fast on its vector instructions. Deploy with TensorFlow Lite Micro or ESP-DL. A keyword-spotting model of about 50k parameters runs inference in a few milliseconds on the ESP32-S3."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "1a26e245-8f9f-fdf7-b8af-fa7e45c8c32e",
    "children": [
     "7d1a87bb-bc6d-830a-fd9d-ec7fe8f96d01"
    ]
   },
   "7d1a87bb-bc6d-830a-fd9d-ec7fe8f96d01": {
    "id": "7d1a87bb-bc6d-830a-fd9d-ec7fe8f96d01",
    "message": {
     "id": "7d1a87bb-bc6d-830a-fd9d-ec7fe8f96d01",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1769368680.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Is it the same quantization as for LLMs?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "14c78a12-7272-9891-50a2-d5e78245b46d",
    "children": [
     "1764b68d-83d7-f8fb-5f68-9992aa9761cb"
    ]
   },
   "1764b68d-83d7-f8fb-5f68-9992aa9761cb": {
    "id": "1764b68d-83d7-f8fb-5f68-9992aa9761cb",
    "message": {
     "id": "1764b68d-83d7-f8fb-5f68-9992aa9761cb",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1769368860.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "The principle is the same, mapping floats to int8 with a scale and zero point, but for microcontrollers you quantize activations too and use integer-only kernels, because there is no fast floating-point path. LLM methods like GPTQ and AWQ focus on 4-bit weights for memory bandwidth; TinyML quantization targets integer arithmetic for compute."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "7d1a87bb-bc6d-830a-fd9d-ec7fe8f96d01",
    "children": []
   }
  },
  "current_node": "1764b68d-83d7-f8fb-5f68-9992aa9761cb",
  "conversation_id": "4ba04d62-548c-001e-bfec-527f500e52fb"
 },
 {
  "title": "Next.js App Router: server components vs client components",
  "create_time": 1762629120.0,
  "update_time": 1762629660.0,
  "mapping": {
   "7d1df936-ff98-c5af-c90e-af169e03818c": {
    "id": "7d1df936-ff98-c5af-c90e-af169e03818c",
    "message": null,
    "parent": null,
    "children": [
     "65fdc251-c4b4-238a-a8c3-b01c3ac5653e"
    ]
   },
   "65fdc251-c4b4-238a-a8c3-b01c3ac5653e": {
    "id": "65fdc251-c4b4-238a-a8c3-b01c3ac5653e",
    "message": {
     "id": "65fdc251-c4b4-238a-a8c3-b01c3ac5653e",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1762629120.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "I'm confused about React Server Components in the Next.js App Router. When do I use 'use client'?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "7d1df936-ff98-c5af-c90e-af169e03818c",
    "children": [
     "7653fbfc-bd8b-935e-59da-da7f3090e7aa"
    ]
   },
   "7653fbfc-bd8b-935e-59da-da7f3090e7aa": {
    "id": "7653fbfc-bd8b-935e-59da-da7f3090e7aa",
    "message": {
     "id": "7653fbfc-bd8b-935e-59da-da7f3090e7aa",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1762629300.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Server Components are the default in the App Router: they render on the server, can fetch data directly (even query the database) and send no JavaScript for themselves to the browser. Add 'use client' only for components that need state, effects, event handlers or browser APIs. A common pattern: a server component page fetches data and passes it as props to small interactive client components."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "65fdc251-c4b4-238a-a8c3-b01c3ac5653e",
    "children": [
     "6de3847a-0b18-cfba-1705-e92bbc6ccf8e"
    ]
   },
   "6de3847a-0b18-cfba-1705-e92bbc6ccf8e": {
    "id": "6de3847a-0b18-cfba-1705-e92bbc6ccf8e",
    "message": {
     "id": "6de3847a-0b18-cfba-1705-e92bbc6ccf8e",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1762629480.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "How do I fetch data with Prisma inside a server component?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "7653fbfc-bd8b-935e-59da-da7f3090e7aa",
    "children": [
     "f9b918fd-bb2b-0f32-ffc1-9eb480f54e96"
    ]
   },
   "f9b918fd-bb2b-0f32-ffc1-9eb480f54e96": {
    "id": "f9b918fd-bb2b-0f32-ffc1-9eb480f54e96",
    "message": {
     "id": "f9b918fd-bb2b-0f32-ffc1-9eb480f54e96",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1762629660.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Import the Prisma client in the server component and await prisma.post.findMany() directly; no API route needed. Instantiate Prisma once (a global singleton in development) to avoid exhausting PostgreSQL connections during hot reload."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "6de3847a-0b18-cfba-1705-e92bbc6ccf8e",
    "children": []
   }
  },
  "current_node": "f9b918fd-bb2b-0f32-ffc1-9eb480f54e96",
  "conversation_id": "389b9189-101e-f80e-d0d6-f88121b36be3"
 },
 {
  "title": "Zero sync engine vs tRPC for a real-time app",
  "create_time": 1768417920.0,
  "update_time": 1768418460.0,
  "mapping": {
   "0f5d70e1-4cbb-6983-b497-6ffd48d78f96": {
    "id": "0f5d70e1-4cbb-6983-b497-6ffd48d78f96",
    "message": null,
    "parent": null,
    "children": [
     "3a59231e-e0e5-aa7a-bf6b-6dfafac3f0c6"
    ]
   },
   "3a59231e-e0e5-aa7a-bf6b-6dfafac3f0c6": {
    "id": "3a59231e-e0e5-aa7a-bf6b-6dfafac3f0c6",
    "message": {
     "id": "3a59231e-e0e5-aa7a-bf6b-6dfafac3f0c6",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1768417920.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "I'm evaluating Zero (the sync engine from Rocicorp) for a collaborative Next.js app. How does it compare to tRPC?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "0f5d70e1-4cbb-6983-b497-6ffd48d78f96",
    "children": [
     "499018e5-2055-1af4-c1f0-ccba80a9e348"
    ]
   },
   "499018e5-2055-1af4-c1f0-ccba80a9e348": {
    "id": "499018e5-2055-1af4-c1f0-ccba80a9e348",
    "message": {
     "id": "499018e5-2055-1af4-c1f0-ccba80a9e348",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1768418100.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "tRPC gives you type-safe request/response APIs: the client calls procedures and you handle caching with React Query. Zero is a sync engine: it keeps a client-side replica of the data you query and syncs changes in real time from PostgreSQL, so reads are instant and updates propagate to all clients automatically. Zero is an alternative to building real-time features on top of tRPC plus websockets. It works with Postgres logical replication."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "3a59231e-e0e5-aa7a-bf6b-6dfafac3f0c6",
    "children": [
     "988c1284-1680-d908-7995-7e460936ef78"
    ]
   },
   "988c1284-1680-d908-7995-7e460936ef78": {
    "id": "988c1284-1680-d908-7995-7e460936ef78",
    "message": {
     "id": "988c1284-1680-d908-7995-7e460936ef78",
     "author": {
      "role": "user"
     },
     "create_time": 1768418280.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "How did I implement Zero sync with Prisma then?"
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "499018e5-2055-1af4-c1f0-ccba80a9e348",
    "children": [
     "31d2dcab-4062-8298-7a18-543c5bd78231"
    ]
   },
   "31d2dcab-4062-8298-7a18-543c5bd78231": {
    "id": "31d2dcab-4062-8298-7a18-543c5bd78231",
    "message": {
     "id": "31d2dcab-4062-8298-7a18-543c5bd78231",
     "author": {
      "role": "assistant"
     },
     "create_time": 1768418460.0,
     "content": {
      "content_type": "text",
      "parts": [
       "Keep Prisma for schema and migrations, and point Zero at the same PostgreSQL database with wal_level=logical. Zero has its own schema definition for the synced tables and permissions; generate it from your Prisma schema so the two stay consistent. Writes go through Zero mutators so they are applied optimistically on the client and confirmed by the server."
      ]
     }
    },
    "parent": "988c1284-1680-d908-7995-7e460936ef78",
    "children": []
   }
  },
  "current_node": "31d2dcab-4062-8298-7a18-543c5bd78231",
  "conversation_id": "2edb3d60-aa70-16f1-83ac-c3fa0f56cd94"
 },
 {
